## The morning the forest smells different

There is one particular morning at the end of summer when the air above Mariánské Lázně changes. After heavy rain, a scent drifts down from the woods above the town that anyone recognises at once: wet moss, decaying leaves and something sweetly fungal beneath it. That is the signal that the loveliest part of the spa year has begun. **Mushroom picking in the Slavkov Forest** is one of the few outings you can complete here before breakfast and still be back in town with a full basket.

This year the signal is unusually strong. At the end of August 2026 the Karlovy Vary Region topped the national Czech mushroom forecast with the highest score of all fourteen regions — the forest around Mariánské Lázně was, quite literally, in the best condition in the country. The main season in the Czech Republic traditionally falls in August and September, though some species carry on well into October.

## The Slavkov Forest starts behind the last spa villa

Mariánské Lázně has an advantage few spa towns can match: you do not have to travel anywhere. The town sits at around 630 metres and is enclosed on three sides by the Slavkov Forest Protected Landscape Area (Slavkovský les, known historically in German as the Kaiserwald). It was designated in 1974 and covers 610 square kilometres — an unbroken ridge of spruce and beech woodland, peat bogs and spring-fed valleys running from Mariánské Lázně all the way to Karlovy Vary.

That geography is what makes autumn here so rewarding. You set off from the colonnade on a waymarked path and twenty minutes later you are in forest where you will not meet another soul. There is no excursion to plan — a basket and a knife will do.

